- **src/components/pages/CheckoutPage/CheckoutForm.tsx** (`CheckoutForm`): This is the site that COMPUTES the mismatch verdict: the confirmEmail FormInput's react-hook-form `validate` rule (lines 305-309) performs the case-sensitive string comparison between the typed confirmEmail value and the watched `email` value, and returns t("TRM774") when they differ. No other module compares the two fields — CheckoutFormProvider.tsx only seeds defaults (email/confirmEmail both set verbatim from user.email, lines 46-59, no normalization) and types.ts (ICheckoutFormValues, lines 3-10) is plain `string` fields with no transform. So the wrong boolean originates here, not downstream.  <== **THIS CHANGE PRODUCES THE VALUE** — it is what makes the new or corrected data exist. Without it, every other change here only moves a value that never changed. [runs: browser]
  - **Minimal fix:** In src/components/pages/CheckoutPage/CheckoutForm.tsx, change the single comparison expression inside the confirmEmail `validate` rule (line 306) from `value !== email` to a case-insensitive comparison: `if (email && value && value.toLowerCase() !== email.toLowerCase()) {`. Nothing else changes — keep the same `t("TRM774")` return and the surrounding truthiness guards. No existing repo helper performs email case-folding (none surfaced by the index), so use the built-in String.prototype.toLowerCase rather than adding a new util. Do NOT mutate the stored form values or add a normalization layer in CheckoutFormProvider.tsx/types.ts — the user's original casing must still be submitted.
- **src/components/pages/CheckoutPage/__tests__/CheckoutForm.spec.tsx** (`CheckoutForm.spec`): Existing spec for this component (helpers getEmail()/getConfirmEmail() at lines 107-108 already target the two fields, and the suite exercises blur-triggered validation via the provider's mode:"onBlur"). It currently has no case-difference scenario, so the regression is uncovered; it needs a new test asserting no TRM774 error when the same address is entered with differing casing.  (verify only — no edit expected here)
  - **Minimal fix:** Add one test that types e.g. "User@Example.com" into getEmail() and "user@example.com" into getConfirmEmail(), blurs, and asserts screen.queryByText("TRM774") is null (mirroring the existing getAllValidationErrors()/blur pattern already used in this file). Reuse the existing getEmail/getConfirmEmail/getAllValidationErrors helpers in this spec; do not add new render harnesses.



## Verification Criteria (what a tester will CONFIRM — your change must satisfy every one)
These are observable checks, derived from the acceptance criteria and description. They describe WHAT is observed, not how to build it. Make the minimal change that makes all of these true; your accompanying test should assert them:
- When the user types the same email address into the Email field in lowercase and into the Confirm Email field in uppercase (or any differing casing) and moves focus away, the Confirm Email field shows no validation error.
- When the user types the same email address into the Email field in uppercase and into the Confirm Email field in lowercase and moves focus away, the Confirm Email field shows no validation error.
- When the user types genuinely different email addresses into the Email and Confirm Email fields (regardless of casing) and moves focus away, the Confirm Email field still shows the existing mismatch error message.
- With matching-but-differently-cased email and confirm-email values entered, the checkout form's submit action is not blocked by a confirm-email mismatch error.

